cted (176):** Whitney leaves only re-rooting. Re-rooting at any junction is legal exactly when deg(o) ≥ 3 and no junction has two loose edges. So these are decided by a one-line criterion, and are unique otherwise, provided the coset test passes.\n- **Overall:** unique 55–70%, same-states-ambiguous 25–35%, undecided 5–15%, concentrated in 2-separation stages with F ≠ C. Different-states rivals: at most a handful, none among the 3-connected.\n- **Bet against:** \"nearly all unique\". Reader ties suggest twist or re-root freedom.\n\n**(e) Separation.** Label each rival R with two bits:\n1. Is span(stars of R) = C? This is a rank check.\n2. Is R = π(G) for some edge permutation π?\n\nIn your population the table-symmetry kind is provably empty. If R = π(G) has table T, then π ∈ Aut(T), which preserves the stars, so R = G. State that as a lemma rather than searching for it.\n\nEvery rival is therefore non-isomorphic to G, and bit 1 alone splits same-states from different-states. Sub-label same-states rivals as re-root, twist, or both, and report rivals up to Aut(T) orbits so counts are not inflated.","ttft_ms":98774,"type":"result","duration_ms":117005,"uuid":"29fa9d34-9b2a-4a4a-995e-c2abeebe10c2","ttft_stream_ms":2569,"time_to_request_ms":81,"first_content_frame_ms":2569,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
